        <detaileddescription>
<para>Pure virtual function that the user overrides to express the interrelation between variables mathematically. A more detailed description of this inheritance process is given in the introduction to the class above.</para>
<sect1 id="namespacecsmp_1implementation">
<title>Implementation</title><para>Within <ref refid="classcsmp_1_1_i_a_p_w_s___h2_o___heat_transfer_velocity_1a0a66d47d3492d5594fabf4b6fa2ae1a9" kindref="member">Calculate()</ref> the user has access to Operands defined in the private data section of the <ref refid="classcsmp_1_1_interrelation" kindref="compound">Interrelation</ref> subclass. These Operands can be used in mathematical and logical expressions, or associated variables can be assigned to local variables of double, scalar, vector or tensor type. Please refer to the documentation of the <ref refid="classcsmp_1_1_operand" kindref="compound">Operand</ref> interface to learn about the overloaded operators. <linebreak/>
</para>
</sect1>
<sect1 id="group___c_s_m_pglobal_enums_1application">
<title>Application Examples</title><para>To calculate local (element-restricted) interdependencies of physical variables inside of the CSP model.</para>
</sect1>
<sect1 id="group___c_s_m_pglobal_functions_1messages">
<title>Messages</title><para>Use the method PrintValue() of the <ref refid="classcsmp_1_1_operand" kindref="compound">Operand</ref> class to output computed property values to screen. </para>
</sect1>
